#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167585} :activity/id #uuid "c294b9cd-2169-5715-a0ea-b56c96da8d7d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049652 :topic/id #uuid "08673d5e-730a-5a5d-82e0-95906c87a855" :topic/math-academy-id 3214} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168066 :tutorial/content "When working with normally distributed random variables, we often want to find a value of $z$ that gives rise to a specific probability. \n\nFor example, suppose that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to find the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z\\leq z) = 0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem is to find a value of $z$ such that $\\Phi(z) = 0.85.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc2006ce351b075c735f9fc1ace31d5688cd39639a6c526810011b44e631be44.png)\n\nA good place to start is the CDF table for $\\Phi(z)$ around the $z$-values where $\\Phi(z)\\approx 0.85.$ This part of the table is shown below.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8485 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8508 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8531 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8708 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8729 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8749 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8907 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8925 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8944 \\,$ |\n\nThere is no listed value of $z$ where $\\Phi(z) = 0.85$ exactly. However, notice that there are values where $\\Phi(z)$ is slightly below and slightly above $0.85{:}$\n\n- $\\Phi(1.03) = 0.8485 < 0.85$\n\n- $\\Phi(1.04) = 0.8508 > 0.85$\n\nTherefore, the required value of $z$ must lie between $1.03$ and $1.04.$ To approximate this value of $z,$ we take the average of these two numbers:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{1.03 + 1.04}{2} = 1.035\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.035) \\approx 0.85.\n$$\n\nTaking the average of two numbers on an interval to find an improved approximation is known as **interval bisection**." :tutorial/id #uuid "2d87d053-8fff-571d-bf83-ee48ea3f1ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4992 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f9c15c99-f0b6-53f9-baac-f0a2a6d399f6" :step/math-academy-id 31092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167665}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166663 :question/id #uuid "a2b50673-6a4f-5e64-ae16-fdce0819637e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8526"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99f2c276-2eb0-5fe9-b46d-6cf30547a96e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050116}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360723}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Z-Score for a Given Probability Using Interval Bisection"} :step/id #uuid "84bdbe07-98a7-5c11-87f9-778014cacb5b" :step/math-academy-id 31695 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166664 :question/id #uuid "d1e64fae-04ef-5c7d-9bb0-e81cf06bb672" :question/math-academy-id "e-8528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a54afd65-a69e-52dd-84eb-7cf936773678" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360725}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"} :step/id #uuid "e0a49c13-605f-56c4-93a7-388766c320b3" :step/math-academy-id 31697 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166673 :question/id #uuid "a29f1f2c-104a-53aa-ad55-0c6abde0a359" :question/math-academy-id "e-8590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8742ad90-1d33-5b56-a83f-8edd7f2f5aef" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411872}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table and Symmetry To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"} :step/id #uuid "4ec11483-e06e-598e-9c74-73dd2d8f63d0" :step/math-academy-id 31898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168083 :tutorial/content "One final way to find the $z$-score for a given probability is to simply look it up in a **percentage points table**, such as the one below.\n\n| $\\quad p \\quad$ | $0.55$ | $0.60$ | $0.65$ | $0.70$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.1257\\,$ | $\\,0.2533\\,$ | $\\,0.3853\\,$ | $\\,0.5244\\,$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\nThis table gives the values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.\n\nLet's use the table above to find $z$ such that $P(Z \\leq z) = 0.75.$\nFirst, we express the given information in terms of $\\Phi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le z) & =0.75 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.75,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np = 0.75 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(p) = 0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75) = 0.6745.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ca3b764-afc6-5490-a58b-a1932c8fc0d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5168 :tutorial/title "Using a Percentage Table To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"} :step/id #uuid "c9e425e7-ceba-5289-97b0-ead6c5ca0320" :step/math-academy-id 32468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167666}}] :activity/title "Percentage Points of the Standard Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166998}]}
